@startuml entity-relationship

' Every domain table, every column, and every foreign key, grouped by the
' same areas reference/schema/schema.md uses (Identity, Catalogue,
' Inventory, Promotions, Cart, Orders, Payments, Shipping, Content,
' Engagement) so this diagram and that page read as one document split two
' ways, not two independent descriptions that can drift apart.
'
' Excluded: Laravel's own framework infrastructure tables (cache,
' cache_locks, sessions, jobs, job_batches, failed_jobs, migrations,
' password_reset_tokens, activity_log) — none of them are domain schema,
' none of them have a foreign key into anything below, and including them
' would dilute a diagram about this project's design with tables every
' Laravel app has by default.
'
' Crow's-foot notation, not Chen: every other diagram in this folder is
' PlantUML, and PlantUML's entity-relationship support speaks crow's-foot
' — introducing Chen's diamond notation here would mean a second toolchain
' and a second visual language for one diagram. Crow's-foot also reads
' 1:1/1:N/N:M directly off the connector shape with no legend needed,
' which is the more useful property for a working schema this size (Chen's
' explicit relationship-diamonds are the stronger choice for *teaching* ER
' modeling on a handful of tables, not for using one at 48 tables).
'
' `linetype ortho` was tried first and rejected: at this entity count it
' produced a single 4096x839 horizontal strip with entities cut off past
' the right edge and connector lines crossing through unrelated boxes —
' technically rendered, not actually readable. Default PlantUML routing,
' used here, lets the layout engine give each entity the vertical space it
' needs instead of forcing everything into orthogonal lanes.
'
' Every column is shown, not a thinned identity-only subset — 384 columns
' across 48 tables, generated mechanically from information_schema.columns
' rather than hand-typed, specifically so a full row-for-row cross-check
' against schema.md or a live database is possible. FK tags come from
' information_schema.KEY_COLUMN_USAGE specifically (not MySQL's COLUMN_KEY
' = MUL alone), because MUL also marks plain non-unique indexes that are
' not foreign keys — roles.name, permissions.name, and orders.email all
' carry MUL for indexing reasons unrelated to any FK.
'
' Verified against the live schema in `amazoff`
' (information_schema.columns for every column and nullability,
' information_schema.KEY_COLUMN_USAGE for every foreign key,
' information_schema.TABLE_CONSTRAINTS for which foreign keys are also
' UNIQUE and therefore 1:1 rather than 1:N) on 2026-09-07, after
' `2026_09_07_190000_derive_payment_status_enum_from_php_enum` — the most
' recent migration at verification time. Regenerate this diagram before
' trusting it against a schema with migrations dated after that one.

hide circle

legend top left
  |= Marker |= Meaning |
  | <b>*</b> before a column | Primary key |
  | <<PK>> | Primary key (composite pivots: multiple per entity) |
  | <<FK>> | Foreign key — verified against KEY_COLUMN_USAGE, not MySQL's MUL alone |
  | <<unique>> | UNIQUE constraint |
  | <<nullable>> | Column allows NULL |
  --
  |= Connector |= Meaning |
  | ||--o{{ | one (required) to many (optional) — standard 1:N, FK not nullable |
  | \|o--o{{ | one (optional) to many (optional) — 1:N, FK nullable |
  | ||--o\| | one to zero-or-one — 1:1, FK unique and nullable |
  | }}o--o{{ | many to many, drawn as two 1:N legs into the pivot table between them |
endlegend

